/*
 * pool-unreferenced — a .deb that crosses the gap without being installable.
 *
 * Protects: core/bundle/unreferenced.go, bundle.UnreferencedFile
 * (last-run-unreferenced.txt) and the pool.unreferenced warning, plus the
 * additive-run rule they exist to report on. This is the fixture
 * test/e2e/harness/scenario.go's prior-build stage names in its own comment
 * ("see fixtures/pool-unreferenced.json") and that did not exist, and it is
 * the only fixture that exercises Request.PriorBuild at all — a mechanism
 * that was implemented and documented with nothing running it.
 *
 * The state it constructs cannot be produced by any single build, which is
 * why the prior-build stage exists. A run is additive and prune never
 * removes the highest version of a (name, arch) group, so a package an
 * EARLIER request named and this one does not has nothing to supersede it
 * and survives in repo/pool regardless of the prune flag. Here: build once
 * for tree, then again — into the same --out directory — for jq.
 *
 * What results is a file with two different answers to "what crossed the
 * gap". tree's .deb is on the media and inside the manifest (manifest.Build
 * walks the whole bundle directory), so it is covered by the operator's
 * signature. It is absent from repo/Packages, which is written from this
 * run's selections alone, and absent from last-run-removed.txt, because
 * nothing removed it. An auditor reading Packages or lock.json is told the
 * media holds strictly less than it does.
 *
 * The fixture asserts both halves of that, and the second half is the one
 * that matters most:
 *
 *   - REPORTED: last-run-unreferenced.txt names tree, and lock.json carries
 *     the pool.unreferenced warning. A file crossing an air gap unlisted is
 *     precisely the thing an offline-bundle tool must not do quietly, and
 *     the report IS the entire mitigation — core/bundle deliberately changes
 *     nothing about what prune removes, so if the report regresses there is
 *     no second line of defence.
 *   - NOT INSTALLABLE: tree must be absent from the freshly installed
 *     target. This is the property that makes the situation safe rather than
 *     merely documented — the target's apt cannot see or select a pool file
 *     repo/Packages does not index. packages_absent, not simply "not in
 *     packages_present", so a regression that indexes or installs the
 *     leftover is actually caught rather than passing unnoticed.
 *
 * Note what the fixture does NOT claim. A substring check cannot prove
 * last-run-unreferenced.txt is otherwise empty, so this asserts the leftover
 * IS reported, not that nothing else is. The stronger claim belongs to
 * core/bundle's own tests, which own the file's exact shape.
 *
 * What would break if this regressed: an operator would carry media whose
 * manifest and signature cover a package their own lock, repository index
 * and run reports all fail to mention — either believing the media holds
 * less than it does, or, if the indexing side regressed instead, installing
 * a package no request ever asked for.
 */
{
  "schema_version": "debark.e2e.fixture/v1",
  "name": "pool-unreferenced",
  "protects": "core/bundle/unreferenced.go; bundle.WarnUnreferenced.7 additive runs; Request.PriorBuild",
  "tags": ["edge", "prune"],
  "target": {
    "distro": "debian",
    "version": "12",
    "arch": "amd64",
    "installed": {}
  },
  "request": {
    "prior_build": { "packages": ["tree"] },
    "packages": ["jq"],
    "sign": true
  },
  "expect": {
    "build": { "exit_class": "success" },
    "packages_present": ["jq"],
    "packages_absent": ["tree"],
    "bundle_files": [
      { "path": "last-run-unreferenced.txt", "contains": ["tree"] },
      { "path": "lock.json", "contains": ["pool.unreferenced"] }
    ],
    "binaries": [
      { "path": "/usr/bin/jq", "args": ["--version"], "exit_code": 0, "stdout_contains": "jq-" }
    ]
  }
}
